# Tenant-Scoped Reads: Trusted Context and Fail-Closed Queries

Business question: can an optional UI filter or another tenant's row ID widen
the authorized result set? This workbook checks the answer against real
PostgreSQL results, not just a generated WHERE clause.

Requires an existing PostgreSQL database via `SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB*`. It uses
the same synthetic, session-local temporary table as the pagination workbook.
No Ecto Repo, migrations, seeds, permanent writes, or schema-per-tenant setup.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_core!()
```

## 1) Keep domain and tenant authority in the application

The fixture domain requires a tenant and always filters `visible = true`.
`trusted_tenant_id` stands for a value resolved by your authenticated host.
It must not come from request parameters or a saved-query/cursor payload.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/workflow_fixture.exs", __DIR__))
connection = SelectoLivebooks.WorkflowFixture.connect!()
runtime = Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, connection)
domain = SelectoLivebooks.WorkflowFixture.domain()
base = Selecto.configure(domain, runtime, mode: :strict)
trusted_tenant_id = 101
```

## 2) Metadata alone is not an applied row filter

Both a missing tenant and a tenant that has only been attached fail validation.
Compilation fails closed until the matching required row filter is applied.

```elixir
assert {:error, %Selecto.Error{type: :validation_error}} = Selecto.validate_tenant_scope(base)
assert_raise RuntimeError, ~r/Tenant scope is required but missing/, fn ->
  base |> Selecto.select(["id"]) |> Selecto.to_sql()
end

attached = Selecto.with_tenant(base, tenant_id: trusted_tenant_id)
assert {:error, %Selecto.Error{}} = Selecto.validate_tenant_scope(attached)

scoped = Selecto.apply_tenant_scope(attached)
assert :ok = Selecto.validate_tenant_scope(scoped)
assert {"tenant_id", 101} in Selecto.required_filters(scoped)
```

## 3) Prove the visible result set

```elixir
identity_query =
  scoped
  |> Selecto.select(["id", "reference"])
  |> Selecto.order_by({"id", :asc})

{:ok, {rows, _columns, _aliases}} = Selecto.execute(identity_query)
assert rows == [[1, "A"], [2, "B"], [3, "C"], [6, "D"]]
{sql, params} = Selecto.to_sql(identity_query)
assert true in params
assert 101 in params
%{sql: sql, params: params, rows: rows}
```

## 4) Optional filters cannot OR away the required scope

Even a predicate mentioning tenant 202 stays inside the required tenant 101
and visibility constraints. An incompatible optional equality returns no rows;
it does not switch tenants.

```elixir
optional_or = Selecto.filter(identity_query, {:or, [
  {"status", "open"}, {"tenant_id", 202}
]})
{:ok, {or_rows, _, _}} = Selecto.execute(optional_or)
assert or_rows == [[1, "A"], [2, "B"], [6, "D"]]

{:ok, {[], _, _}} =
  identity_query |> Selecto.filter({"tenant_id", 202}) |> Selecto.execute()

{:ok, {[], _, _}} =
  identity_query |> Selecto.filter({"id", 5}) |> Selecto.execute()
```

This demonstrates defense in depth, not a recommendation to accept arbitrary
filter ASTs from clients. Expose a small, typed filter allowlist or authorized
query-library views at your application's boundary.

## 5) Reject conflicting trusted context and caller-authored SQL

```elixir
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn ->
  Selecto.apply_tenant_scope(scoped, tenant_id: 202)
end

mismatched = Selecto.require_tenant_filter(attached, "tenant_id", 202)
assert {:error, %Selecto.Error{details: %{code: :tenant_scope_mismatch}}} =
  Selecto.validate_tenant_scope(mismatched)

assert_raise Selecto.PolicyViolation, fn ->
  Selecto.select(scoped, {:raw_sql, "current_user"})
end

other_tenant_query =
  base
  |> Selecto.with_tenant(tenant_id: 202)
  |> Selecto.apply_tenant_scope()
  |> Selecto.select(["id", "reference"])

{:ok, {[[5, "OTHER-TENANT"]], _, _}} = Selecto.execute(other_tenant_query)
{:ok, {^rows, _, _}} = Selecto.execute(identity_query)
```

Build each request from the trusted base; do not mutate or recycle a scoped
query across tenants. Required root scope is not automatic authorization of
every possible joined table. Design relationship keys and tenant policies
together (including composite tenant keys where needed), and use database
roles/RLS when appropriate. Strict mode is not a sandbox for arbitrary BEAM
code. These examples prove only the stated read cases, not the whole app.

## 6) Clean up

```elixir
:ok = GenServer.stop(connection)
```

For writes, use the separate [Updato tour](selecto_updato_feature_tour.livemd)
and its domain-owned operation, field, relationship, and cardinality policies.
